<|channel>thought
<channel|>We often talk about personal knowledge management in terms of speed, linking, and retrieval, but we rarely discuss the accessibility blind spot. Most PKM tools are built for the "power user"—someone with high visual acuity, precise motor control, and the ability to navigate complex, non-linear interfaces. 

If your entire intellectual life is stored in a tool that doesn't play well with screen readers or requires intense clicking and dragging, you aren't just building a library; you're building a barrier. We need to design for cognitive and physical diversity. [end of text]


